contrib/pg_bp_arc/README

pg_bp_arc -- Adaptive Replacement Cache (ARC)
==============================================

pg_bp_arc provides the ARC buffer replacement algorithm as a PostgreSQL
extension.  ARC automatically balances between recency-friendly and
frequency-friendly caching by observing which evicted pages get
re-requested, and adjusting its internal allocation accordingly.

No tuning knobs are required.  The algorithm adapts to workload changes
on its own.


Installation
============

pg_bp_arc is a contrib extension.  After building PostgreSQL with this
contrib module, load the extension and create a buffer pool:

    CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_arc;

    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_arc_pool
        HANDLER arc_pool_handler
        SIZE '256MB';

Assign tables or indexes to the pool:

    CREATE TABLE hot_data (id int, val text)
        WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_arc_pool');

    ALTER TABLE existing_table SET (buffer_pool = 'my_arc_pool');

To use ARC as the default buffer pool algorithm for all pools, add
pg_bp_arc to shared_preload_libraries in postgresql.conf:

    shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_bp_arc'

This registers ARC as the BP_ALGO_ARC algorithm, available for the
DEFAULT pool and any pool created with HANDLER arc_pool_handler.


When to Use ARC
===============

ARC is a good fit for:

  - Workloads with shifting hot/cold data (the algorithm adapts
    automatically as the working set changes)
  - Mixed recency/frequency access patterns (OLTP with periodic
    analytical or reporting scans)
  - Tables that benefit from scan-resistant caching (large sequential
    scans do not flush the entire cache)
  - VACUUM-heavy workloads (VACUUM pages are given low priority to
    avoid polluting the cache)

ARC may not be the best choice for:

  - Very high concurrency on many-core systems, where the single
    spinlock becomes a bottleneck (consider pg_bp_car instead)
  - Workloads that benefit from prefetch-aware caching (ARC does not
    implement the prefetch_hint callback; CAR does)


How It Works (Brief)
====================

ARC maintains four logical lists:

    T1   Recently accessed pages (accessed once since entering cache)
    T2   Frequently accessed pages (accessed two or more times)
    B1   Ghost entries for pages recently evicted from T1
    B2   Ghost entries for pages recently evicted from T2

On a cache hit in T1, the page is promoted to T2.  On a hit in T2,
the page moves to the most-recently-used end of T2.

On a cache miss, ARC checks the ghost lists:

  - B1 hit:  T1 was too small.  Increase the T1 target size.
  - B2 hit:  T2 was too small.  Decrease the T1 target size.
  - Neither: Complete miss.  Allocate a new entry on T1.

The eviction decision follows the adaptive target: if T1 has reached
or exceeded the target size, evict from T1; otherwise evict from T2.


VACUUM Integration
==================

When VACUUM is active, newly loaded pages are inserted at the
least-recently-used (LRU) end of T1 rather than the MRU end.  This
ensures VACUUM-loaded pages are the first candidates for eviction,
preventing cache pollution from maintenance operations.


Statistics
==========

Install the extension to get the pg_stat_arc view:

    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_arc;

The view returns one row per ARC-managed pool with these columns:

    Column           Type    Description
    ------           ----    -----------
    name             name    Pool name
    oid              oid     Pool OID (NULL if not cataloged)
    t1_size          int4    Current number of pages on T1
    t2_size          int4    Current number of pages on T2
    b1_size          int4    Current number of ghost entries on B1
    b2_size          int4    Current number of ghost entries on B2
    target_t1_size   int4    Current adaptive T1 target
    lookups          int8    Total buffer lookups (hits + misses)
    t1_hits          int8    Hits on T1 (promoted to T2)
    t2_hits          int8    Hits on T2 (moved to T2 MRU)
    b1_hits          int8    Ghost hits on B1 (T1 target increased)
    b2_hits          int8    Ghost hits on B2 (T1 target decreased)
    misses           int8    Complete cache misses
    t1_evictions     int8    Pages evicted from T1
    t2_evictions     int8    Pages evicted from T2

Example: check the hit ratio and adaptation balance:

    SELECT name,
           t1_size, t2_size, target_t1_size,
           CASE WHEN lookups > 0
                THEN round(100.0 * (t1_hits + t2_hits) / lookups, 2)
                ELSE 0
           END AS hit_pct,
           b1_hits, b2_hits
    FROM pg_stat_arc;


Size Advisory Function
======================

pg_bp_arc_size_recommendation returns sizing guidance for a pool:

    SELECT *
    FROM pg_bp_arc_size_recommendation('my_arc_pool')
        AS (current_size int,
            recommended_size int,
            ghost_pressure float8,
            hit_ratio float8);

    Column             Type     Description
    ------             ----     -----------
    current_size       int      Current pool size in buffers
    recommended_size   int      Suggested pool size in buffers
    ghost_pressure     float8   (b1_hits + b2_hits) / misses
    hit_ratio          float8   (t1_hits + t2_hits) / lookups

Interpretation:

  - ghost_pressure > 0.1 indicates the pool is too small.  Pages are
    being evicted and then accessed again shortly after.  The
    recommended_size will be larger than current_size.

  - ghost_pressure near 0 means evicted pages are not being
    re-requested, so the pool size is adequate.

  - hit_ratio close to 1.0 means most accesses are served from cache.

The recommended_size is a heuristic: current_size scaled up by
ghost_pressure, capped at 2x current_size.


Limitations
===========

Single spinlock
    Every hit and miss acquires arc_lock.  This limits scalability on
    high-core-count systems with very high buffer access rates.  For
    workloads where this is a bottleneck, consider pg_bp_car, which
    uses atomic reference bits instead of a spinlock for hit tracking.

No prefetch integration
    ARC does not implement the prefetch_hint callback.  Use CAR if
    prefetch-aware caching is needed.

2x CDB memory overhead
    ARC allocates two Cache Directory Block entries per physical buffer
    (one for the buffer itself, one for a potential ghost entry).  This
    adds roughly 100-120 bytes per buffer of overhead.

Patent notice
    The ARC algorithm is covered by IBM patent US 6,996,676.  The CAR
    algorithm (pg_bp_car) was specifically designed as a patent-free
    alternative.


Reference
=========

N. Megiddo and D. Modha, "ARC: A Self-Tuning, Low Overhead Replacement
Cache", Proceedings of USENIX FAST 2003.

https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast-03/arc-self-tuning-low-overhead-replacement-cache
